"""Original thematic dossiers. Paragraph headings are also matching-task keys.

Each block is a separate communication, not a renaming of a shared story.
No external test content is redistributed.
"""
FOUNDATION_SECOND = [
('班级留言板','A shared table','A place to meet~Our English group meets at a table near the window. There are four chairs. We put our names on small cards. The cards help new students remember us.||Different jobs~Ana works in a hotel. Ben is a driver. I work at home. We have different jobs, but we all want to speak to customers in English.||One simple question~At the start, we ask one question: How are you today? We listen to the answer. We do not read a long speech from a phone.||Helping a new member~Today a new member sits with us. He does not have a card. Ben gives him some paper. Ana asks his name and shows him our notebooks.'),
('安排学习时间','A small change to my evening','An old habit~I often look at my phone after dinner. Ten minutes becomes an hour. My English book stays in my bag, and I feel too tired to open it.||A visible reminder~Now I put the book on the kitchen table before dinner. I also leave a pencil beside it. I can start without looking for anything.||A realistic target~I study for fifteen minutes first. I read five sentences and say them without the book. I do not try to finish a whole chapter every evening.||A shared routine~My sister studies with me on Thursdays. On other days I work alone. We send each other one sentence at the weekend and talk about what was difficult.'),
('办公室物品','The missing charger','A problem before work~The office laptop has very little power. Its charger is not in the drawer. A meeting starts in twenty minutes, so Sam asks his colleagues for help.||Finding the right person~Rita used the laptop yesterday. She says the charger is in the training room, beside the printer. Sam checks that room, but the door is locked.||Getting access~The receptionist has a spare key. She opens the room for Sam. The charger is exactly where Rita said it was, under a sheet of paper.||Preventing another search~Sam puts a label on the charger. After the meeting he returns both items to the drawer. He also writes a short message about where shared equipment belongs.'),
('买菜与预算','Enough for three meals','Planning before shopping~Lena looks in her cupboard before she goes shopping. She already has rice and oil. She writes down eggs, tomatoes, beans and one small bag of onions.||Comparing quantities~A large bag of tomatoes costs less per tomato. However, Lena lives alone and cannot use them all. She buys a smaller bag to avoid wasting food.||Changing the menu~The shop has no fresh beans today. Lena chooses frozen beans instead. They cost the same and can stay in the freezer until she needs them.||Checking the total~At home she checks the receipt. She has enough money left for fruit. She plans three meals using the ingredients rather than buying a different set for each meal.'),
('交通信息','Two ways to get there','The usual journey~I normally take bus twelve to the sports center. It stops outside my home. The journey is short, but the bus is often full after school.||An unexpected change~Today a notice says the road near the center is closed. Bus twelve stops two streets away. Passengers must walk the rest of the way.||A different route~My friend suggests taking the train. The station is closer to the entrance, but a train ticket costs more. We decide to walk from the temporary bus stop.||Leaving some extra time~We leave home fifteen minutes earlier than usual. We arrive before our class starts. Next week we will check the travel notice again before setting out.'),
('问路与地标','A visitor at the library','Starting at the entrance~A visitor asks where she can print a document. The library assistant points to the stairs. The printers are upstairs, opposite the computers, not beside the entrance.||An accessible alternative~The visitor has a heavy bag. The assistant explains that there is also a lift behind the information desk. She does not need to carry the bag upstairs.||Paying for printing~A black and white page costs ten cents. The machine accepts a library card. Visitors without a card can buy a temporary printing ticket at the desk.||Checking before leaving~The visitor prints one page first to check the size. Then she prints the rest. She collects her original document and signs out of the public computer.'),
('预约与健康','Before the appointment','Making the appointment~Tom calls the clinic about a sore knee. The nurse offers Tuesday morning. He works then, so he asks whether there is an appointment after four in the afternoon.||Confirming the time~The nurse finds a space at four thirty on Wednesday. Tom repeats the day and time. He writes them on his calendar before ending the call.||What to bring~The clinic asks him to bring a list of any medicines he takes. He should also arrive ten minutes early to complete a short form at reception.||When plans change~If he cannot attend, he should call the clinic. The nurse says another patient may need the appointment. Sending a message to a friend does not cancel the booking.'),
('住房规则','Sharing a kitchen','Agreeing on space~Three people share our apartment. Each person has one shelf in the cupboard. We write our names on containers so nobody takes the wrong lunch to work.||Keeping it clean~The person who cooks washes the pans. We clean the floor together on Sunday. A short cleaning list on the fridge helps everyone remember the arrangement.||Talking about noise~One housemate leaves for work very early. She prepares her breakfast the night before. This means she does not need to use the noisy mixer at six.||Reviewing the agreement~After a month we talk about the rules. The cleaning list works well, but we need more space for vegetables. We move the drinks to another shelf.'),
('周末活动','A rainy Saturday','An outdoor plan~Our family wants to have lunch in the park. We pack sandwiches and a ball. Before we leave, dark clouds appear and the weather report predicts heavy rain.||Choosing indoors~We decide to visit the local museum. Entry is free, and there is an area where children can draw. We can eat our sandwiches in the covered courtyard.||Something unexpected~At the museum we find a display about old buses. My father remembers taking one of them to school. He tells us how tickets looked in those days.||A useful lesson~We enjoy the afternoon even though our first plan changes. Next time we arrange an outdoor activity, we will think of an indoor choice before the day begins.'),
('阶段复习：信息核对','A message for the group','The original arrangement~The walking group usually meets outside the station at nine. Members do not need to book, but new walkers should introduce themselves to the leader before the walk.||A new meeting point~This Saturday the station entrance is under repair. The group will meet at the cafe across the road instead. The starting time remains nine, not nine thirty.||Preparing for the walk~The route takes about two hours. Bring water and comfortable shoes. There is a steep part near the river, so the leader suggests leaving heavy bags at home.||Checking understanding~The leader asks members to reply to the message. A reply confirms that they have seen the change. It does not reserve a seat or pay for refreshments.'),
('过去经历','My first volunteer shift','An uncertain start~I joined a charity shop last month. On my first morning I arrived early because I was nervous. The manager showed me where to leave my coat and bag.||Learning the system~We sorted books before opening. Prices depended on the type and condition of each book. I put damaged books in a separate box instead of placing them on the shelves.||Asking for help~A customer wanted to return a jacket. I did not know the policy, so I asked the manager. She checked the receipt and explained the available options.||A more confident finish~By lunchtime I could welcome customers and find the correct shelf. I still needed help with payments. The manager suggested practising the payment system before my next shift.'),
('旅途中的意外','The wrong platform','A rushed departure~We reached the station shortly before our train was due. The departure board showed several services to the same city. I looked only at the destination and hurried downstairs.||Noticing a difference~On the platform, my friend checked the departure time against our tickets. This train left earlier and stopped at different stations. We realized we were waiting in the wrong place.||Finding reliable information~A member of staff showed us the correct platform. It was on the other side of the station. She told us to use the footbridge rather than leave the building.||Changing our routine~We caught the right train with a few minutes to spare. Now we check the service number as well as the destination. We also allow time to ask for help.'),
('未来安排','Planning a small celebration','Working around schedules~Our colleague is leaving at the end of the month. We want to arrange a meal, but several people work evening shifts. We ask everyone about two possible dates.||Choosing a place~Most people can come on Friday. We choose a restaurant near the office so nobody needs a car. It has vegetarian dishes and enough space for the whole group.||Sharing responsibilities~I am going to book the table. Maya will collect messages for a card. We agree on a spending limit before buying a gift so nobody feels uncomfortable.||Keeping a backup~One colleague may have to work late. We tell the restaurant that the final number could change. On Thursday we will confirm attendance and send everyone the meeting time.'),
('经验与变化','Learning to cycle again','An earlier difficulty~I learned to ride a bicycle as a child, but I stopped after a fall. For many years I avoided cycling, even when my friends invited me on short trips.||A careful return~Recently I have started lessons in a quiet school yard. My instructor checks the brakes and helmet before each session. We practise stopping before we try longer distances.||Progress so far~I have completed four lessons. I can now turn and stop without putting both feet down too early. Busy roads still worry me, and I have not ridden in traffic yet.||The next step~The instructor suggests a protected cycle path for our next lesson. I will go with her first. My aim is a safe journey to the shops, not a fast race.'),
('学习习惯','Why I changed my notes','Too much copying~At first I copied every unfamiliar word into a notebook. The pages looked neat, but I rarely opened them again. During conversations I still could not use the words.||A different record~Now I record short phrases from things I read or hear. Beside each phrase, I write one sentence about my own life. This helps me connect meaning with a situation.||Testing recall~Three days later I cover the English and try to say the phrase. If I cannot remember it, I listen again and make a new example instead of copying the old one.||Checking real use~Once a week I tell a friend about my week. I try to use two of the phrases naturally. A phrase that I recognize on paper may still need more speaking practice.'),
('比较选择','Choosing a language class','Two possible courses~The community center offers a large evening class and a smaller weekend group. Both use the same textbook. The weekend group costs more because there are fewer students.||Looking beyond price~I work some Saturdays, so the weekend option is difficult for me. The evening class is larger, but it meets on a day when I am usually free.||Asking about practice~I ask the teacher how students practise speaking. She says the evening class uses pairs and changes partners often. Students do not spend the whole lesson listening to a lecture.||Making a practical decision~I choose the evening course and arrange one short practice call with a classmate each week. The more expensive course might be useful, but I am more likely to attend this one regularly.'),
('礼貌请求','Asking for a quieter room','A problem at night~During a hotel stay, I could hear building work outside my window late in the evening. I had an early meeting and needed to sleep. Closing the window did not help much.||Explaining clearly~I called reception and described the noise and my room number. Instead of demanding a refund immediately, I asked whether a room on the other side of the building was available.||Considering the offer~The receptionist offered a quieter room on a lower floor. It was smaller, but the price was the same. I asked whether I could see it before moving my bags.||Confirming the change~After checking the room, I accepted it. Reception updated my key card and arranged help with my luggage. I thanked the staff and checked that my breakfast booking was still included.'),
('条件与备选','An outdoor class','A useful location~Our art tutor plans a drawing class beside the river. The location has trees, boats and an old bridge. These give students different shapes and textures to practise drawing.||A weather condition~If heavy rain is forecast, the class will move to the studio. A message will be posted by seven in the morning. Light rain alone does not automatically cancel the outdoor session.||Bringing the right things~Students should bring a sketchbook, pencils and a waterproof cover. Paint is not needed. There are no shops at the meeting point, so bring drinking water as well.||Missing a message~Anyone without internet access can phone the studio after seven. The tutor asks students not to guess the location based only on the weather outside their own homes.'),
('服务反馈','A delivery that arrived late','What was promised~I ordered a desk for delivery on Monday afternoon. The confirmation email said the driver would call thirty minutes before arrival. I arranged to work from home that day.||What actually happened~The desk did not arrive until Tuesday morning. Nobody called on Monday to explain the delay. The desk itself was in good condition, but I had missed an important meeting while waiting.||A specific request~I wrote to customer service with my order number and the two dates. I asked for the delivery charge to be refunded. I did not request a replacement desk because it was undamaged.||The company response~The company apologized and refunded the delivery charge. It also said its tracking system had failed to send an update. I kept the email until the refund appeared in my account.'),
('阶段复习：方案与限制','A community garden application','Available spaces~The neighborhood garden has twelve small plots. Residents without private gardens can apply first. People who already have a garden may join a waiting list if spaces remain after April.||Responsibilities~Members must water their own plants and help maintain shared paths. The annual fee pays for communal tools and water. Seeds are not included, and members choose what to grow.||A practical restriction~Tall plants must not block neighboring plots. Chemical weed killers are not allowed because children use the garden. Members should ask the coordinator before building any permanent structure.||Making an application~Applicants complete a short form describing when they can visit. Plots are not assigned simply to the first twelve people who apply. The coordinator also checks whether applicants can meet the maintenance requirements.')
]

# Late-course dossiers: week, theme, title, four independently authored texts.
DOSSIERS=[]
def dossier(week,theme,title,a,b,c,d): DOSSIERS.append((week,theme,title,a,b,c,d))

dossier(21,'志愿服务','A rota that works',
'The original rota~A food bank assigned volunteers to fixed weekly shifts. This made staffing predictable, but people working variable hours frequently cancelled. The coordinator wanted fewer last-minute gaps without excluding those volunteers.||A trial arrangement~For six weeks, volunteers could offer availability every fortnight. Two experienced volunteers stayed on each shift, while flexible places changed. New volunteers were paired with someone familiar with food safety procedures.||An unexpected problem~The flexible rota reduced cancellations but increased messages. Some volunteers assumed that submitting availability meant a shift was confirmed. The coordinator introduced a separate confirmation email showing the assigned date and role.||Judging the trial~At the review meeting, staff compared unfilled shifts and time spent arranging cover. They also asked volunteers about stress. A system that saved cancellations but doubled administrative work would need further adjustment.',
'A volunteer speaks~I could not promise every Tuesday because my employer changes my hours. The new system let me help twice this month. I waited for confirmation before booking my bus ticket.||What training changed~On my first shift I learned that food must be checked before shelving. A clean package is not enough: the date and storage instructions also matter. I asked an experienced volunteer to check uncertain items.||A customer interaction~One visitor could not carry a heavy bag. I offered to divide the food between two smaller bags. I did not ask personal questions about why the visitor needed help.||A suggestion for the future~I suggested putting routine instructions beside the packing tables. New volunteers could check them without interrupting everyone. The coordinator agreed, but said written instructions would not replace the initial safety briefing.',
'The briefing begins~Coordinator: We expected eight volunteers on Saturday, but two are unavailable. We will pack fewer parcels in the morning and ask the afternoon team to finish the rest.||A role changes~Volunteer: I was assigned to reception. Should I switch to packing? Coordinator: Stay at reception until ten. Then Jo will take over, and you can help at the packing table.||A delivery constraint~Driver: The fresh vegetables will arrive at eleven instead of nine. Coordinator: Leave space in the cold store. Do not put them beside the canned food while waiting for instructions.||Checking the arrangement~Volunteer: So I move at ten, and vegetables arrive at eleven? Coordinator: Exactly. Tell me if Jo is delayed; reception must never be left unattended.',
'A separate service~A clothing charity accepts donations on weekday mornings. Weekend volunteers sort items already received; they cannot accept new bags because the collection room is closed.||Useful donations~Clean coats and sturdy shoes are especially needed. Broken electrical items are not accepted, even if donors offer to pay for repairs. The charity has no electrical testing equipment.||Booking a collection~Home collection is available for large donations within the district. A request does not guarantee a collection date. Staff first check whether a vehicle and two volunteers are available.||A changing need~During the winter campaign, the charity suspends book donations to create space for coats. This temporary rule will be reviewed in February rather than automatically becoming permanent.')

dossier(22,'职场培训','Learning a new system',
'A costly shortcut~A warehouse introduced handheld scanners to replace paper lists. Experienced staff initially entered several items together after packing. This felt faster, but made it difficult to trace where a wrong item had entered an order.||A different sequence~The trainer asked staff to scan each item before putting it in a box. A warning appeared immediately if the code did not match. The change slowed the first few orders while preventing some later corrections.||Measuring fairly~Managers compared error rates for similar orders rather than all orders combined. Large orders naturally took longer, so average packing time alone could make a careful worker look inefficient.||Support after training~A supervisor remained available during the first week. Workers recorded confusing messages on a shared sheet. The software team then revised two warnings that used technical language staff had not been taught.',
'An employee reflection~I had packed orders for years and thought training would be unnecessary. During practice I discovered that a familiar product now had two different sizes. Looking at the color of the packaging was no longer enough.||A useful mistake~I deliberately scanned the wrong size in the practice area. The screen showed a warning and would not close the order. Trying the mistake safely helped me understand the protection built into the system.||Helping a colleague~A colleague found the small text difficult to read. We asked for a larger display setting. The trainer treated this as an access issue, not evidence that my colleague lacked ability.||What remained difficult~The scanner sometimes lost its connection near the loading door. We learned the approved offline procedure and reported each incident. Inventing our own workarounds would have made stock records less reliable.',
'A training invitation~Manager: Tomorrow\'s scanner session starts at eight thirty in the practice room. Bring your staff card, but leave personal phones in your locker.||A timetable conflict~Worker: My delivery arrives at nine. Can I attend the afternoon session? Manager: Yes, it starts at two. Please tell the trainer so we can prepare enough devices.||A practice requirement~Worker: Will we handle customer orders? Manager: No, all boxes are marked training. We want you to practise mistakes without affecting real deliveries.||After the session~Manager: Complete the practice order before you return to work. If the connection fails, call the trainer. Finishing quickly is less important than following the correct sequence.',
'A booking system~A dental practice is replacing its appointment software on Friday evening. Existing appointments will transfer automatically, but handwritten notes must be checked by reception staff before the old system closes.||During the change~Online booking will be unavailable overnight. Patients can leave a voicemail, although this does not reserve an appointment. Staff will return calls when the new system opens on Saturday.||Staff preparation~Receptionists practise using fictional patient records. They must not copy real medical information into the training system. The trainer supplies sample names and contact details.||Reviewing errors~For the first week, a supervisor checks cancelled appointments each afternoon. The aim is to identify misunderstandings early, not to punish staff for asking questions.')

dossier(23,'公共交通','When a timetable changes',
'A service problem~The last bus from the industrial estate left before many evening shifts ended. Workers without cars either paid for taxis or waited for friends. The council received requests for a later service.||A limited experiment~Rather than immediately adding buses all night, the operator tested one departure at ten fifteen for three months. Employers agreed to display information and count how many staff might use it.||Interpreting passenger numbers~The first week was quiet because workers had already arranged lifts. By the fourth week, passenger numbers had risen. Evaluating only the opening week would therefore have underestimated demand.||A financial decision~Ticket income did not cover the full cost, but two large employers offered partial support. The council considered access to employment alongside revenue. It still required evidence that the service was being used regularly.',
'A passenger diary~On Monday I tried the new late bus. It reached my stop five minutes after the printed time. I worried that I had misunderstood the timetable, so I checked the direction on the stop sign.||A useful conversation~The driver explained that temporary roadworks were delaying the service. He advised passengers to use the operator\'s live information where possible. He could not promise the same delay every night.||Planning a connection~My connecting bus leaves only ten minutes after the scheduled arrival. I now check whether there is enough time before buying a combined ticket. A cheap journey is not useful if the connection is regularly missed.||Making feedback specific~I sent the operator the route number, date and actual arrival time. I asked whether the timetable allowed for the roadworks. A specific report was more useful than simply saying the buses were always late.',
'An announcement~Driver: This service will finish at Central Square tonight because of a road closure. Passengers for the hospital should change to the shuttle outside the library.||A question about tickets~Passenger: Will I need to pay again? Driver: No. Show your current ticket to the shuttle driver. Keep it until you finish your journey.||Finding the stop~Passenger: Is that the normal library stop? Driver: No, the temporary stop is behind the building. Follow the yellow signs from the square.||A final connection~Driver: The final shuttle leaves at ten forty. Our expected arrival is ten twenty, so there should be time to transfer. Please speak to me if you require assistance.',
'A ferry trial~A river ferry will carry bicycles free of charge during a summer trial. Passengers still need an ordinary ticket. Electric cargo bikes are excluded because they exceed the deck\'s size limit.||Capacity matters~Bicycle spaces cannot be reserved. Staff load pedestrians first and then assess remaining space. Arriving early improves the chance of boarding but does not guarantee a place for a bicycle.||Bad weather~Strong winds may suspend the service even on dry days. Travellers should check the morning notice rather than assume sunshine means normal operation. Unused tickets remain valid for a later sailing.||Giving feedback~The operator wants reports of journeys that could not be completed, including attempts to board a full boat. Counting successful trips alone would hide unmet demand.')

dossier(24,'社区设施','Keeping a library useful',
'A changing need~The library received complaints about noise and a separate request for more group activities. Staff recognized that these demands were not necessarily contradictory: different visitors needed different spaces at different times.||A zoning trial~The upstairs room became a quiet study area. Conversation was permitted downstairs, where children\'s events already took place. Signs explained the distinction without suggesting that families were less welcome.||Access and capacity~The quiet room had limited seats and was reached by a lift as well as stairs. Staff checked whether visitors using the lift could enter independently. A quiet area that some people could not reach would not solve the problem fairly.||Reviewing the outcome~After six weeks, complaints had fallen but afternoon seating remained scarce. The next proposal concerned opening hours rather than stricter noise rules. Staff used visitor counts as well as written complaints.',
'A student\'s experience~I used to study at home beside a busy road. When the library opened its quiet room, I began going there twice a week. I reserve difficult reading tasks for those visits.||Sharing limited space~One afternoon every seat was occupied, although two desks held only coats. I asked staff about the policy. They explained that short breaks were allowed but belongings could not reserve a seat for the whole day.||A small access problem~The room\'s door was heavy. A visitor using a walking aid had difficulty opening it. We reported this, and the library installed a button that operated the door.||Why both spaces matter~I sometimes use the downstairs tables to practise speaking with a friend. The quiet room is better for reading, but silence everywhere would remove another valuable reason for using the library.',
'A room request~Visitor: Can I book a room for a language exchange on Thursday? Librarian: Room Two is available from six until seven thirty. It holds eight people.||Equipment needs~Visitor: We may need a screen. Librarian: That room has no screen, but there is a whiteboard. Room One has a screen and is already booked.||A cost question~Visitor: Is there a charge? Librarian: Community groups can use the room free if attendance is free. Paid classes use a different booking rate.||Finishing the booking~Librarian: Please give us a contact number and leave ten minutes at the end for tidying. Your booking ends at seven thirty; cleaning is included in that time.',
'A leisure center notice~The center is opening a quiet exercise session on Sunday mornings. Music will be turned off, and attendance will be limited. The session is open to any adult who prefers a calmer environment.||Booking and payment~Members must reserve a place despite having unlimited entry. Non-members pay the usual single-session fee. No extra charge applies for choosing the quiet session.||Available facilities~The swimming pool follows its ordinary schedule and is not included in the quiet arrangement. The notice applies only to the exercise room on the first floor.||Evaluating demand~Staff will record waiting-list numbers as well as attendance. A session with full attendance every week may still be too small if many people cannot obtain a place.')

dossier(25,'消费与维修','Repair or replace?',
'An apparent bargain~A family\'s washing machine stopped draining after six years. A shop advertised a discounted replacement, but the family first asked a repairer for an inspection. Buying immediately would have avoided the inspection fee but also removed the chance of a cheap repair.||The diagnosis~The repairer found a damaged pump; the drum and motor were working. Replacing the pump would cost much less than a new machine. However, the repairer could not guarantee how long unrelated components would last.||Comparing the options~The new model used less electricity, but the saving depended on how often it ran. The family compared the extra purchase cost with realistic annual savings rather than assuming an efficient label made replacement automatically cheaper.||A conditional choice~They chose repair because the main components were sound and they used the machine only a few times a week. If the motor had also failed, their decision might have been different.',
'A repair cafe visit~At a community event I brought a lamp that flickered. Volunteers asked when the problem occurred and whether the cable had been damaged. They unplugged it before examining any parts.||A limit on help~The volunteer explained that they could inspect simple faults but could not certify every appliance as safe. If specialist testing was needed, they would recommend a qualified repairer rather than guess.||Learning something practical~The fault was in the switch. I watched the repair and learned how to recognize a loose connection without touching exposed wiring. Watching a demonstration did not make me qualified to repair other electrical devices alone.||The wider value~The event saved one lamp from disposal and helped me understand its condition. Its value was not only financial. It also made me more likely to seek advice before throwing away a repairable object.',
'A repair call~Customer: My oven heats unevenly. Can someone visit this week? Adviser: We have a slot on Friday morning. The inspection fee is forty pounds.||Clarifying the price~Customer: Does that include parts? Adviser: No. The engineer will give you a separate quote before fitting anything. You can decline the repair after the inspection.||Preparing the visit~Adviser: Please send the model number from the label inside the door. A photograph is fine. Do not move the oven yourself if it is connected to a fixed gas supply.||Confirming consent~Customer: So I pay for inspection even if I refuse the repair? Adviser: Yes. No repair work will start without your agreement to the quote.',
'A shoe repair offer~A repair shop gives free estimates for bags and shoes brought to the counter. Postal estimates require a delivery charge, which is not refunded if the customer decides against repair.||Material limits~The shop can replace most heels and zips. Some synthetic materials cannot be stitched securely. Staff inspect the material before promising a result.||Turnaround times~Simple heel repairs normally take two working days. Specialist parts may take longer to arrive. The collection date is confirmed only after the parts are available.||After collection~Customers should check the repair before leaving. If a repaired seam fails within thirty days under normal use, the shop will examine it without another assessment fee.')

dossier(26,'工作沟通','Making meetings shorter',
'An overloaded calendar~A small design team held a meeting every morning, but many updates repeated information already available online. Staff said the meetings interrupted their most productive work without helping them resolve difficult decisions.||Separating purposes~The manager moved routine progress reports to a shared board. A shorter meeting remained for decisions requiring discussion. Anyone requesting discussion had to explain the decision needed, not merely name a broad topic.||A participation concern~Quieter team members initially contributed less in the shorter format. The manager began circulating questions the day before and invited written comments. Faster meetings were not automatically more inclusive.||Checking what improved~The team tracked unfinished decisions and repeated discussions, not just minutes saved. A short meeting that produced confusion could cost more time later. They kept one longer session each fortnight for complex planning.',
'A practical agenda~Before a supplier meeting, I wrote three questions about cost, delivery and technical support. I shared them in advance. This gave the supplier time to bring accurate information instead of making guesses during the call.||Recording decisions~During the meeting I noted what was agreed, who would act and by when. I separated confirmed decisions from possibilities. Otherwise, a suggestion could later be mistaken for a commitment.||Checking disagreement~When two people described the deadline differently, I read both versions aloud. We agreed that delivery meant arrival at our office, not departure from the warehouse. That distinction changed the proposed date.||Following up~I sent a short summary and asked for corrections by the next afternoon. The document did not replace the contract, but it helped everyone identify misunderstandings while the conversation was still fresh.',
'The meeting opens~Chair: We have twenty minutes today. The first decision is whether to delay the product demonstration. The supplier can deliver the sample on Thursday, one day later than expected.||A constraint appears~Sales: The client is only available on Friday morning. If we postpone to next week, we will lose that slot. Technician: I need half a day to test the sample.||An alternative~Chair: Could we prepare the software now and test the hardware Thursday afternoon? Technician: Yes, provided delivery is before noon. Later delivery would leave too little time.||The action~Chair: I will ask the supplier to confirm a morning delivery in writing. We will keep Friday provisionally and make the final decision when that confirmation arrives.',
'A residents\' meeting~The housing association will discuss parking permits on Monday. Residents may submit questions beforehand. Submitting a question does not guarantee speaking time, because similar questions may be grouped together.||Access arrangements~The meeting is in the ground-floor hall. A live audio link is available for residents unable to attend. Remote participants must register to receive the link.||Decision boundaries~The meeting will collect views rather than approve a final policy. A written proposal will follow, with a separate period for responses. Existing permits remain valid in the meantime.||The written record~Minutes will summarize decisions and actions, not reproduce every comment word for word. Residents may request correction of factual errors in the published summary.')

dossier(27,'餐饮与食品','Understanding a food label',
'Two different dates~A community cooking course began by comparing labels. A use-by date concerns safety when food is stored as instructed. A best-before date mainly concerns quality. Treating them as identical can lead either to waste or unsafe decisions.||Storage changes the picture~A printed date assumes particular storage conditions. Leaving chilled food in a warm car may make it unsafe before that date. Smell alone cannot establish whether every food is safe to eat.||Planning portions~Participants measured how much rice they usually cooked and compared it with what remained. Smaller first portions, with extra food available if needed, reduced leftovers without requiring anyone to go hungry.||Evaluating the lesson~The tutor asked learners to explain a label in their own words and plan a shopping list. Repeating a definition was easier than applying it when dates, quantities and storage instructions appeared together.',
'A household experiment~We kept a simple record of food we threw away for two weeks. Bread and salad appeared most often. We had expected expensive ingredients to dominate, but small regular losses added up.||Changing one purchase~We began freezing half a loaf immediately after buying it. The bread could then be toasted from frozen. This was more reliable than waiting until it was already stale.||A different problem~Salad required another solution because we did not enjoy it after freezing. We bought smaller quantities and planned meals using the same vegetables on consecutive days.||A cautious conclusion~Our waste fell during the next fortnight, but we were also eating at home more often. We could not attribute every improvement to the shopping changes. We continued recording waste to see whether the pattern lasted.',
'A cooking class question~Learner: Can I use this yogurt? The best-before date was yesterday. Tutor: First read the storage instructions and check its condition. Do not confuse that label with a use-by date.||A safety distinction~Learner: What about this chilled chicken? Tutor: It has a use-by date. Follow that date and the storage instructions; looking or smelling normal is not a reliable safety test.||Planning the meal~Tutor: Today we will cook vegetables first and keep raw chicken separate. Use a clean board for ready-to-eat food. Wash your hands after handling raw meat.||Storing leftovers~Learner: Shall I leave everything on the table until evening? Tutor: No. Follow the food-safety guidance supplied with the class for prompt cooling and refrigeration.',
'A catering order~A workplace caterer requires final lunch numbers by ten on the previous working day. Orders for Monday therefore need confirmation on Friday. Late additions are subject to available stock.||Dietary information~Customers must identify allergies separately from general preferences. A vegetarian request does not tell the kitchen whether a person has a milk allergy. Staff may contact the organizer for clarification.||Delivery conditions~Cold items arrive in insulated boxes. The organizer must provide suitable storage if food will not be served immediately. The delivery driver cannot inspect every meeting room\'s facilities.||Returning equipment~Reusable boxes are collected the next day. Missing boxes incur a replacement charge. Disposable plates are optional and must be ordered separately.')

dossier(28,'旅行住宿','What a booking includes',
'An attractive headline~A guesthouse advertised a low nightly price. Before booking, a traveller checked whether breakfast, local tax and parking were included. Comparing headline prices alone would not show the full cost of either accommodation option.||An arrival restriction~Reception closed at nine in the evening. Late arrival was possible only if the guest requested a key code in advance. A delayed train would not automatically notify the guesthouse.||The cancellation terms~The cheapest rate was non-refundable. A slightly higher rate allowed cancellation until two days before arrival. Because the traveller\'s work schedule was uncertain, flexibility had a real value.||Choosing on total value~The traveller selected the flexible rate and used public transport. Another guest arriving by car might have preferred a different property. The best option depended on individual needs rather than one universal ranking.',
'A host\'s message~Thank you for booking our apartment. The address is on your confirmation. The entrance is beside the bakery, not through it. Please avoid blocking the bakery door while unloading luggage.||Using shared space~The courtyard is shared with residents. You may sit there during the day, but quiet hours begin at ten. Laundry may be dried on the folding rack inside the apartment only.||Reporting a problem~If something is missing or damaged, send us a photograph and a short description. This helps us bring the correct replacement. Please report urgent water leaks by phone instead of waiting for an email reply.||Checking out~Leave the keys in the box by eleven and close the windows. You do not need to wash bed linen. Luggage storage after checkout is possible only by prior arrangement because the next guests may arrive early.',
'An arrival call~Guest: My train is delayed. I will arrive around ten thirty. Host: Reception closes at nine, but I can send you a key code if you confirm your booking number.||Clarifying access~Guest: Does the code open the room? Host: It opens the box beside the entrance. Your key and directions to the room are inside.||A breakfast question~Guest: Is breakfast available early? Host: The dining room opens at seven. If you leave before then, request a breakfast bag by eight this evening.||Final confirmation~Guest: Please arrange the bag. I leave at six forty-five. Host: I will leave it in the shared fridge with your room number. The key code will arrive by text shortly.',
'A campsite notice~Tent pitches include space for one vehicle. Additional cars must use the visitor parking area and pay a daily fee. Vehicles cannot remain on the grass beside other pitches.||Arrival times~New arrivals may check in after two. Early arrivals can use the cafe, but cannot occupy a pitch until cleaning and inspection are complete. Vacant appearance does not mean a pitch is ready.||Fire restrictions~Raised cooking stoves are permitted unless a fire warning is in force. Open fires and disposable barbecues placed directly on the ground are prohibited throughout the season.||Cancellation~Bookings cancelled at least a week ahead receive a refund minus the administration fee. The fee is also retained when guests shorten their stay after arrival.')

dossier(29,'学习策略','Practising retrieval',
'A familiar illusion~A learner read the same vocabulary list repeatedly and found it increasingly easy to recognize. However, when asked to use the words in a conversation, she hesitated. Familiar appearance had been mistaken for reliable recall.||An alternative task~She began covering the English and producing a phrase from a situation. The task felt harder than rereading, even though it took no longer. Difficulty during practice did not necessarily mean the method was ineffective.||Spacing the practice~Instead of repeating everything ten times in one evening, she returned to selected phrases on later days. Forgotten phrases received earlier review. Already reliable phrases appeared less often, leaving time for other skills.||Testing transfer~She then used the phrases in a new conversation topic. Successful recall from a list was useful, but flexible use required another step. Her progress record separated recognition, recall and independent expression.',
'A study partner\'s rules~My partner and I agreed not to interrupt every small error during a conversation. Constant correction had made us afraid to finish a sentence. We now record two important issues for discussion afterwards.||Making feedback usable~Instead of writing "grammar is bad", we keep the original sentence, a corrected version and a short explanation. The following day, the speaker uses the pattern in a different situation.||Avoiding memorized performances~We occasionally practise the same topic twice, but change the follow-up questions. A smooth repeated speech may show preparation rather than the ability to respond spontaneously.||Knowing our limits~We can help each other notice hesitation and unclear organization. Neither of us claims to be an official examiner. When using AI feedback, we ask it to cite our actual words and distinguish evidence from guesses.',
'Planning a review~Learner: I have fifty words due today. Should I finish all of them before speaking practice? Coach: Spend eight minutes on the most difficult items, then continue with your speaking task.||Handling unfinished work~Learner: Will the rest disappear? Coach: No, they remain due. Reduce new vocabulary today rather than extending the session indefinitely.||Choosing useful examples~Learner: Can I just repeat the example sentence? Coach: First understand it, then make a sentence about your own experience. Check that the meaning still fits.||Checking improvement~Coach: Tomorrow, try recalling the phrase without looking. A tick beside today\'s page shows completion, not permanent mastery.',
'A revision workshop~Students bring one piece of writing with feedback. They first identify the main communication problem before correcting spelling. A perfectly spelled paragraph can still fail to answer the question.||Peer discussion~Partners explain where meaning became unclear. They do not rewrite the whole essay for each other. The original writer remains responsible for choosing and understanding revisions.||A second attempt~Writers produce a new paragraph on a related topic without copying the corrected version. This tests whether they can apply the lesson beyond a single familiar sentence.||Record keeping~Both versions are saved with dates. The workshop does not assign an IELTS band because the short task does not provide enough evidence across the official criteria.')

dossier(30,'阶段检查：综合信息','Opening a community workshop',
'A proposed workshop~Residents wanted a shared place to repair furniture. A vacant shop was available, but its low rent did not include heating, insurance or alterations. The planning group calculated these costs before deciding whether membership fees would be sufficient.||Managing different risks~Members could use hand tools after an induction. Powered equipment required separate training and a supervisor on site. Previous experience did not automatically exempt someone from learning the workshop\'s procedures.||A reservation system~Large benches could be reserved for two-hour blocks. Members were asked to cancel unused bookings so others could attend. Repeated failure to cancel would temporarily limit advance booking, not end membership permanently.||Evaluating public benefit~The group planned to record items repaired, member participation and costs. They also wanted to know whether new users felt welcome. A workshop used only by a small group of experts might not meet its community purpose.',
'A funding application~The organizers requested money for access improvements and essential safety equipment. They did not ask the council to pay all running costs indefinitely. Membership income was expected to cover ordinary operation after the first year.||Evidence of demand~A survey showed strong interest, but expressions of interest were not the same as paid memberships. The organizers ran a trial weekend and recorded actual attendance to strengthen their forecast.||A realistic timetable~Building alterations needed approval before work could begin. The proposed opening month was therefore provisional. Advertising a definite date too early could create commitments the group could not meet.||An accountability plan~Quarterly reports would compare spending with the approved budget and explain major changes. The council would receive summaries without members\' private contact details. Transparency about funds did not require publishing personal information.',
'The planning meeting~Organizer: We hoped to open in June, but the entrance alterations will not finish until July. We can still run demonstrations at the summer fair.||A funding question~Treasurer: Can we use the equipment grant to pay rent during the delay? Organizer: Not without the funder\'s written agreement. It was awarded for specified purchases.||A training offer~Volunteer: I can teach furniture repair on Saturdays. Organizer: Thank you. Please attend the safety briefing first, even though you have professional experience.||The next action~Organizer: We will revise the timetable this week and contact registered members. We should explain the reason for the delay rather than leave the old opening date online.',
'A photography club~The club is offering a darkroom course for beginners. Students need no previous darkroom experience, but must complete an online safety introduction before the first practical session.||Included materials~The fee includes chemicals and ten sheets of photographic paper. Additional paper can be purchased during the course. Cameras are not provided, although negatives for practice are available.||Attendance limits~Only six students can use the darkroom at once. A place is confirmed after both payment and the safety introduction are complete. Paying alone does not complete registration.||Missed sessions~A missed practical session may be repeated with another group if space is available. The club cannot guarantee an individual replacement lesson or refund for personal absence.')
